# Keep the build context small and the image honest.
#
# .venv matters most: it is a few hundred megabytes of Windows binaries that would be
# useless in a Linux image and, while metas-unclib was still a dependency, would have
# carried a licensed library into the build context. .gitignore already keeps it out of
# git, so a hosting provider building from the repository never sees it; this is what
# keeps a local `docker build` equally clean.

.git
.gitattributes
.github

.venv
.pytest_cache
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info

# Generated and working directories, both already gitignored.
out
temp

tools/node_modules
tools/package*.json

# Not part of the running application. The Dockerfile copies pyproject.toml, uv.lock,
# README.md and src and nothing else, so none of these reach the image in any case;
# excluding them keeps the build context honest about that rather than sending the
# journal and the design record to the daemon on every build. PLAN.md is a build
# journal, firstPrompt.md is the prompt that started the project, and ARCHITECTURE.md is
# a design record -- no route serves any of them, and an earlier note here said a later
# change set would serve ARCHITECTURE.md from the site, which never happened. README.md
# stays: pyproject.toml declares it as the project readme, so the build reads it.
ARCHITECTURE.md
PLAN.md
firstPrompt.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
render.yaml
